      "text": "The first few rows of the display are shown here: $1 \\mathbf{1},$ $1 \\mathbf{3},$ $1 \\mathbf{5},$ $1 \\mathbf{7},$ $1 \\mathbf{9},$ $2 \\mathbf{1},$ $2 \\mathbf{3},$ $2 \\mathbf{5},$ $2 \\mathbf{7},$ $2 \\mathbf{9} \\quad(10 \\text{ rows})$\n\nThe next row, $3 \\mathbf{1},$ will have $3 + 2 = 5$ cans:  $ 1 \\mathbf{1},$ $1 \\mathbf{3},$ $1 \\mathbf{5},$ $1 \\mathbf{7},$ $1 \\mathbf{9},$ $2 \\mathbf{1},$ $2 \\mathbf{3},$ $2 \\mathbf{5},$ $2 \\mathbf{7},$ $2 \\mathbf{9},$ $3 \\mathbf{1},$ $3 \\mathbf{3},$ $3 \\mathbf{5},$ $3 \\mathbf{7},$ $3 \\mathbf{9} \\quad(12 \\text{ rows})$\n\nThe next row, $4 \\mathbf{1},$ will have $4 + 2 = 6$ cans:  $ 1 \\mathbf{1},$ $1 \\mathbf{3},$ $1 \\mathbf{5},$ $1 \\mathbf{7},$ $1 \\mathbf{9},$ $2 \\mathbf{1},$ ... $3 \\mathbf{1},$ ... $4 \\mathbf{1},$ $1 \\mathbf{1}$, indeed $12$ rows later\n\nThe $13^{\\text{th}}$ row has 9 cans, the $14^{\\text{th}}$ row has 11 cans, and so on.\n\nThe total number of cans is then $$1 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot 3 + 1 \\cdot 5 + 1 \\cdot 7 + 1 \\cdot 9 + 2 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot 3 + 2 \\cdot 5 + \\dots + 9 \\cdot 1$$\n\nWhich, by adding these row vectors together, is\n\n$$1(1+3+5+7+9) + 2(1+3+5+7+9) + 3(1+3+5+7+9) + \\dots + 9(9)$$\n\n$$ = 10(1+3+5+7+9) + 4 \\cdot \\frac{9(10)}{2}\\text{ cans}$$\n\nSince we know that the display has $100$ cans, we assign this equation to $\\mathbf C = 10 \\times 10 + 45 = \\mathbf 145.$\n\nThe currents row, 19 are $19(1+2+3\\dots+9)$ (the triangular numbers); i.e. $$19(1+2+3+\\dots+9) + 4 \\cdot \\frac{9(10)}{2}$$which is equal to $19(1+2+3+\\dots+9) + 180 = 19(55) + 180 = 199.$",
