      "text": "Let's denote the number of rows by \\( n \\). Then we can calculate the total number of cans in the display. \nThe number of cans in the top row is 1.\nThe number of cans in the second row is 3.\nThe number of cans in the third row is 5.\nAnd so on, the number of cans in each row goes up by 2.\n\nSo, we can list out the number of cans in each row: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15, ..., \\( 2(n-1) \\). \n\nThe number of cans in the \\( n \\)-th row is therefore \\(2(n-1)\\).\n\nThe total number of cans in the display is the sum of the first \\( n \\) natural numbers, an arithmetic series. We know the sum of the first \\( n \\) natural numbers is given by the formula \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\nSince the total number of cans is 100, we have the equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2} = 100\n\\]\n\nThe number \\( n \\) represents the number of rows. Solving this equation, we get:\n\\[\nn(n+1) = 200\n\\]\n\\[\nn^2 + n - 200 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation:\n\\[\nn = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 + 800}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{801}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThe numerical value of \\(\\sqrt{801} \\approx 28.3\\). Thus,\n\\[\nn = \\frac{-1 + 28.3}{2} \\approx 13.65\n\\]\n\nSince the number of rows \\( n \\) must be an integer, we round down to the nearest whole number.\n\nHence, the number of rows is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}\n\\]",
